## Priority 1B: Desktop Academic Knowledge Platform

> [!NOTE]
> The emotional hook is not the coin price. It is the *story*. "In 1804, this dollar passed through the hands of a merchant in Philadelphia. Jefferson had just been re-elected. The Louisiana Purchase had just been completed."
> That is what makes Numista.AI irreplaceable.

### Platform Architecture
- **Primary:** Browser-based web app, designed for 1280px+ desktop screens
- **Secondary:** Responsive mobile web (same codebase, reduced layout)
- **Tertiary:** Flutter companion app (existing work, reframed)

### Core Desktop Features

#### The Collection Dashboard
- Full-screen grid/table view of the collection
- Rich filtering: by year, mint, series, grade, purchase price, acquisition date
- Sort by value, rarity, historical significance
- Visual "completeness" view per series (e.g., Morgan Dollars: 47/97 complete)

#### The Coin Record Page (The Heart of the Product)
Each coin in the collection gets a dedicated, Wikipedia-depth record page:
- **Obverse & Reverse images** (high-res, zoomable)
- **The Story** — AI-written historical narrative: what was happening in America, who designed it, why this denomination existed
- **Mint Data** — Mintage figures, die varieties, known errors
- **Provenance** — Auction history, known previous owners (where public)
- **Your Record** — When you acquired it, what you paid, grade, notes
- **Market Context** — Recent eBay/auction sales (eBay API — see note below)
- **Related Coins** — "If you own this, you may be interested in..."

#### The Research Assistant (AI Chat, Desktop-First)
- Multi-turn research sessions with full session history
- Supports complex questions: "Compare the strike quality of 1881-S vs 1881-O Morgan Dollars and explain why the difference exists"
- Cites sources: mintage records, Red Book, ANA standards
- Agent Skills (compact knowledge files per series) prevent context bloat

#### The Knowledge Base (Public, SEO-Indexed)
- Every US coin program has a public, citable reference page
- Replaces "just Googling" for coin history
- This is how Numista.AI becomes the academic authority — not by having an app, but by *being the reference*

### eBay API Integration Note
This belongs here, not in an "Appraiser Agent." Framing matters:
- ❌ "What is my coin worth?" → Appraisal app territory
- ✅ "Recent auction results for this coin type" → Academic/market research
The data is identical; the context makes Numista.AI the reference, not the valuator.

